<p><a id=3D"editing"></a></p>
<h2>6. Edit for understanding, then add polish</h2>
<p>Editing decides what the viewer experiences and in what order. A useful =
cut removes confusion, makes an example visible, or preserves the rhythm of=
 a thought. An effect that does none of those things is optional.</p>
<p>Use one editor for the first six videos. This guide uses <strong>DaVinci=
 Resolve=E2=80=99s free version</strong> as the default. Some current AI fe=
atures require Studio or specific hardware; none are required here. Blackma=
gic=E2=80=99s current site identifies Resolve 21, while some official train=
ing files still refer to version 20. Core operations carry across, but butt=
on names and panels can vary. Use the lessons labeled Introduction to Editi=
ng and Introduction to Audio first. <a href=3D"https://www.blackmagicdesign=
.com/products/davinciresolve/training">Official training and practice files=
</a>.</p>
<p>If your computer struggles even with small 1080p files, use a simpler ed=
itor you already have. Learning the sequence below is more important than a=
dopting a particular brand.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Set up the project once</h3>
<p>Create folders named Footage, Audio, Graphics, Exports, and Project. Cop=
y your source files into them before importing. Give the project a clear na=
me such as <code>V01-Course-To-Project</code>. In project settings, set the=
 timeline to the actual recording frame rate and 1920=C3=971080 for the ini=
tial workflow. Resolve may offer to match the source frame rate on import; =
decide before substantial editing.</p>
<p>Import the footage through the Media or Edit page. The media pool is you=
r library of source files. The timeline is the arranged sequence that will =
become the video. A video track holds picture; an audio track holds sound. =
Keep spoken audio on one or two clearly named tracks so you can adjust it c=
onsistently.</p>
<p>If playback stutters, generate proxy media or use the editor=E2=80=99s l=
ower-resolution playback options. Proxies are smaller working copies linked=
 to the originals; they are not an excuse to delete the originals. Do not s=
pend a full session color grading a clip that cannot play smoothly yet.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Pass one: assemble the argument</h3>
<p>Place the best full take of each section in order. Use the viewer to fin=
d the beginning and ending of the useful part, then trim or split around it=
. Remove a failed take only after checking that the replacement contains th=
e whole sentence.</p>
<p>A <strong>ripple delete</strong> closes the gap left by a removed sectio=
n. It is useful for removing a failed start, but check the downstream timin=
g. Clips placed on other tracks may shift unexpectedly depending on selecti=
on and track settings. Review every join rather than assuming the operation=
 did what you intended.</p>
<p>Listen to the assembly without music or B-roll. Does the claim lead to a=
n example, then a method, then a limit and action? If not, fix the order. A=
 finished spoken assembly is often called an A-cut or radio edit: the core =
explanation works through sound before decoration.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>Pass two: make the speaking rhythm comfortable</h3>
<p>Trim distracting pauses, repetitions, and verbal detours. Retain enough =
silence to let an important sentence land. Cutting every breath can make a =
calm lesson feel anxious. If one point is repeated three times, keep the cl=
earest version rather than hiding the repetition under different camera cro=
ps.</p>
<p>A jump cut is an obvious change within the same camera angle. It is acce=
ptable when it removes dead time without damaging meaning. If it interrupts=
 a gesture awkwardly, cut at a more natural pause or cover the join with a =
relevant demonstration.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-4"></a></p>
<h4>Recipe 1: a gentle change of framing</h4>
<p>For a new section, select the clip and adjust Zoom and Position in the I=
nspector for a slightly tighter composition. A small crop can distinguish a=
 new beat. If the source is only 1080p, large crops will soften it; do not =
rely on the ability to magnify indefinitely. A genuine wider shot or full-s=
creen notebook is often a better alternative.</p>
<p>Use a crop when the thought changes, not on a timer. There is no evidenc=
e-based requirement to zoom every few seconds.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-5"></a></p>
<h3>Pass three: add visuals that answer a question</h3>
<p>Put supporting footage on a video track above the talking head while lea=
ving narration below. Trim the support clip to the part of the sentence it =
explains. If it contains irrelevant camera noise, mute or remove that clip=
=E2=80=99s audio without deleting the main narration.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-6"></a></p>
<h4>Recipe 2: cover a cut with an actual demonstration</h4>
<p>Suppose you say, =E2=80=9CWrite a finish line you can recognize,=E2=80=
=9D then cut to the next sentence. Place a shot of writing that finish line=
 above both sides of the cut. The speech remains continuous, and the viewer=
 sees the action being described. The shot earns its place even if the unde=
rlying cut were invisible.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-7"></a></p>
<h4>Recipe 3: a J-cut or L-cut</h4>
<p>A J-cut starts the next scene=E2=80=99s sound before its picture. An L-c=
ut lets the previous scene=E2=80=99s sound continue after the picture chang=
es. For example, continue your sentence while showing the notebook; that is=
 an L-cut in this context. To create one, separate picture and audio trimmi=
ng carefully or place B-roll above continuous narration. Keep lip-sync inta=
ct when returning to the speaker.</p>
<p>These are continuity tools, not effects you must use in every scene. If =
separating clips makes the timeline confusing, use the simpler B-roll-above=
-narration method first.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-8"></a></p>
<h4>Recipe 4: a clean three-step graphic</h4>
<p>Create a plain background using a solid-color generator or an image you =
made. Add a basic Text title for =E2=80=9CFinish line,=E2=80=9D then anothe=
r for =E2=80=9CNext action,=E2=80=9D then =E2=80=9CReview.=E2=80=9D Align t=
hem consistently and reveal each when you mention it. Static appearance or =
a short fade is enough.</p>
<p>If using motion, keyframe Position or Zoom between a starting and ending=
 value. A keyframe stores a value at a specific time, allowing the editor t=
o interpolate between them. Keep the movement modest and stop it before the=
 viewer needs to read. Do not learn an elaborate Fusion animation before yo=
u can make a readable static card.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-9"></a></p>
<h4>Recipe 5: a truthful results chart</h4>
<p>If your actual record contains eight planned evenings and five starts, s=
how those counts directly. Label the observation period and definition of a=
 start. A simple row of eight boxes may be clearer than a dramatic rising l=
ine. If numbers are illustrative, label them =E2=80=9Cillustrative=E2=80=9D=
; do not let a polished chart imply measurement you did not perform.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Pass four: make speech easy to hear</h3>
<p>Open the audio mixer or Fairlight page. Balance clip levels so the viewe=
r does not need to change volume between sections. Avoid red clipping indic=
ators. If one word is unusually loud, adjust that section rather than crush=
ing the entire track.</p>
<p>Use equalization only to solve a problem you can hear. A gentle high-pas=
s filter can reduce low rumble; setting it too high can thin the voice. Com=
pression reduces the difference between loud and quiet portions; excessive =
compression can emphasize room noise or make speech tiring. Start with a li=
ght voice preset if available, compare before and after at similar volume, =
and trust the recording over the preset=E2=80=99s name.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-11"></a></p>
<h4>Recipe 6: keep music behind the voice</h4>
<p>For R videos, begin without music. For P videos, add it only if it impro=
ves the sequence. Lower music beneath speech and fade it at transitions. Yo=
u can automate volume with points on the audio clip or track. There is no u=
niversal fader number that guarantees intelligibility because source tracks=
 have different loudness. Check on a phone speaker at low volume.</p>
<p>YouTube=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/3=
376882?hl=3Den">Audio Library</a> is a useful starting source. Check each t=
rack=E2=80=99s license and include required attribution. Keep the attributi=
on text and track details with the project. =E2=80=9CRoyalty-free=E2=80=9D =
does not mean every item found elsewhere is unrestricted.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>Pass five: match the image and add accessibility</h3>
<p>Correct obvious brightness and white-balance differences before applying=
 a stylized look. Skin should look plausible and important text should stay=
 readable. Do not add a strong color preset to fix poor exposure; it often =
makes the problem more noticeable.</p>
<p>For captions, use a tool available to you or YouTube=E2=80=99s automatic=
 caption draft, then correct names, punctuation, and misleading word errors=
. Captions are valuable for accessibility. Full-screen animated word-by-wor=
d subtitles are an aesthetic choice, not a substitute for an available corr=
ected caption track. If you use burned-in captions, keep them away from imp=
ortant on-screen objects and interface controls.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Export and inspect the actual file</h3>
<p>For the initial 1080p SDR workflow, export an MP4 using H.264 video and =
AAC audio, at the source frame rate. YouTube lists 8 Mbps as a reference bi=
trate for standard-frame-rate 1080p SDR; an appropriate editor preset can a=
lso be a practical starting point. Use 48 kHz audio. These are delivery set=
tings, not a requirement to change already-recorded speech to achieve a par=
ticular number. <a href=3D"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/172217=
1?hl=3Den">YouTube upload settings</a>.</p>
<p>Play the exported file, not just the timeline. Check the start, a middle=
 section, all new graphics, and the end. Watch the whole file before publis=
hing when practical. An export can reveal missing media, black frames, trun=
cated audio, or unreadable text that the editing preview did not make obvio=
us. Upload privately or unlisted for processing checks before making it pub=
lic. Verify the processed playback and captions.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Use AI on specific bottlenecks</h3>
<p>An assistant can critique a script for vague claims, suggest alternative=
 openings, or turn your outline into a shot checklist. Give it your actual =
evidence and instruct it not to invent experience or research. Treat every =
factual addition as unverified until checked.</p>
<p>Audio enhancement may help a difficult recording, but it can produce met=
allic sound or alter speech. <a href=3D"https://podcast.adobe.com/en/enhanc=
e">Adobe Enhance Speech</a> is one optional service to investigate; its pag=
e was reachable but plan details were not available in readable research ou=
tput. No free quota or account access is assumed here. Use a short comparis=
on before relying on any enhancer. Keep the untouched recording and avoid u=
ploading sensitive material unnecessarily.</p>
<p>Automatic clipping, generative B-roll, and avatar tools are not prioriti=
es for your first six videos. They can increase output without improving th=
e idea. Do not generate fake evidence of a personal experiment. Spend saved=
 time on real examples and better explanations.</p>
<p><a id=3D"editing-section-15"></a></p>
<h3>Stop rules for both formats</h3>
<p>For R: one clean spoken assembly, useful demonstration, balanced voice, =
corrected captions, and an inspected export. For P: the same foundation plu=
s the small planned set of visuals and matching results. When the timebox i=
s exhausted, remove optional effects. Never remove the evidence check or sp=
eech check to make room for animation.</p>
