<p><a id=3D"production-playbooks"></a></p>
<h2>Two repeatable production playbooks</h2>
<p>These are original workflows informed by public creator examples. They a=
re not claims about either creator=E2=80=99s exact current private process.=
</p>
<p><a id=3D"production-playbooks-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>R =E2=80=94 direct advice</h3>
<p><strong>Use when:</strong> one bounded opinion or method can help a spec=
ific viewer. Initial scope: 3=E2=80=937 minutes. Basic production estimate:=
 5 hours 15 minutes, with additional learning and research time available i=
n the fortnightly plan.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick the situation.</strong> Write who is watching and what dec=
ision they need to make. A subject such as =E2=80=9Cconfidence=E2=80=9D is =
not enough.</li>
<li><strong>Make one claim.</strong> State the answer in one sentence. Remo=
ve a second major thesis or save it for the next video.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare six cards.</strong> Situation =E2=86=92 claim =E2=86=92=
 example =E2=86=92 method =E2=86=92 limit =E2=86=92 action. Write the openi=
ng and ending exactly if that helps.</li>
<li><strong>Check the evidence.</strong> Mark experience, opinion, and rese=
arch separately. Replace invented history with a demonstration or prospecti=
ve test.</li>
<li><strong>Set the phone.</strong> Landscape, eye height, soft light, quie=
t background, stable support, close speech recording.</li>
<li><strong>Review a short test.</strong> Listen for echo, clothing noise, =
clipping, and the correct microphone input. Check focus and exposure.</li>
<li><strong>Record in sections.</strong> Pause after mistakes, restart the =
whole sentence, and keep useful takes. Capture the example and thumbnail ph=
oto before resetting the room.</li>
<li><strong>Assemble the voice.</strong> Keep the best complete sections. C=
ut failed starts and duplicate points; retain natural breathing and meaning=
ful pauses.</li>
<li><strong>Add only useful support.</strong> One notebook demonstration or=
 short method card may be sufficient. Music is optional.</li>
<li><strong>Finish and publish.</strong> Balance voice, correct captions, i=
nspect the export and processed upload, and match title to promise.</li>
<li><strong>Review at planned checkpoints.</strong> Record a 48=E2=80=9372-=
hour technical/audience check, a seven-day snapshot, and a later comparison=
 if enough data exists.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Stop editing when:</strong> the argument is complete, speech is =
clear, the example is understandable, and the export passes review. Decorat=
ive effects do not need another evening.</p>
<p><a id=3D"production-playbooks-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>P =E2=80=94 polished teaching or experiment</h3>
<p><strong>Use when:</strong> showing a process, comparison, or real observ=
ation improves the lesson. Initial scope: 5=E2=80=938 minutes, planned with=
in a 17-hour fortnight.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Choose the question.</strong> Name what the viewer will underst=
and or be able to try afterward.</li>
<li><strong>Plan the evidence.</strong> For an experiment, define the metho=
d and measurements before starting. Save failed attempts too. For a demonst=
ration, label illustrative material accurately.</li>
<li><strong>Write seven beats.</strong> Question =E2=86=92 constraint =E2=
=86=92 plan =E2=86=92 attempts =E2=86=92 complication =E2=86=92 finding =E2=
=86=92 application. Omit a beat that does not genuinely apply; do not inven=
t a crisis.</li>
<li><strong>Write a two-column script.</strong> Narration on one side, nece=
ssary visuals on the other. Read aloud and time it.</li>
<li><strong>Limit the visual package.</strong> Plan approximately eight pur=
poseful supporting shots and three reusable graphic styles. This is a plann=
ing allowance, not an editing formula.</li>
<li><strong>Record the main lesson.</strong> Use soft controlled light and =
close speech audio. Record section by section. Get pickups and the thumbnai=
l before dismantling.</li>
<li><strong>Capture supporting shots.</strong> Film the real notebook, task=
, calendar, result, and process. Reenactments must not imply evidence they =
do not supply.</li>
<li><strong>Make the spoken assembly.</strong> Fix the story before adding =
visuals.</li>
<li><strong>Add demonstrations and cards.</strong> Each visual must answer =
a question, show evidence, or clarify a transition. Replace costly animatio=
n with a readable still if needed.</li>
<li><strong>Polish speech and picture.</strong> Balance audio, place music =
quietly if useful, match brightness/color, and correct captions.</li>
<li><strong>Audit truth and usability.</strong> Match claims to the log, nu=
mbers to the actual record, and title to the conclusion. Read graphics on a=
 small screen.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect and publish.</strong> Check the exported file, processe=
d upload, captions, sources, and next step. Record the time spent by stage.=
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>When over budget:</strong> remove custom animation, an extra sce=
ne, or an optional Short. Keep the truthful explanation, speech check, and =
export inspection.</p>
<p><a id=3D"production-playbooks-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>A ten-minute weekly review</h3>
<p>Record what you finished, what took longer than expected, one repeated v=
iewer question, one production improvement, and the next concrete action. I=
f analytics are sparse, say so. Keep a sustainable promise and make the nex=
t piece clearer.</p>
